before:
Assignment can update an outer let binding.
```javascript
let mood = "quiet";
if (true) {
  mood = "curious";
}
console.log(mood);
```
This prints curious.

A new declaration with the same name creates a separate inner binding. This is called shadowing.
```javascript
let mood = "quiet";
if (true) {
  let mood = "curious";
  console.log(mood);
}
console.log(mood);
```
The inner log prints curious; the outer log prints quiet.

after:
Without another `let`, assignment changes the existing name:
```javascript
let mood = "quiet";
if (true) {
  mood = "curious";
}
console.log(mood);
```
Output:
```text
curious
```

Another `let` creates a separate inner name. This is called shadowing:
```javascript
let mood = "quiet";
if (true) {
  let mood = "curious";
  console.log(mood);
}
console.log(mood);
```
Output:
```text
curious
quiet
```
The outer `mood` still refers to `"quiet"`.

